 ### [Marching Tetrahedra](1733-marching-tetrahedra.md)
-Marching Tetrahedra (MT) is an isosurface extraction algorithm that decomposes each cubic voxel into tetrahedra to resolve the topological ambiguities present in Marching Cubes. By processing tetrahedra independently, it guarantees a manifold mesh without cracks.
+Marching Tetrahedra (MT) is an isosurface extraction algorithm that decomposes each cubic voxel into tetrahedra to resolve the topological ambiguities present in Marching Cubes. By processing tetrahedra independently, it guarantees a manifold mesh without cracks. Choose it when topological correctness and manifold guarantees are critical.
 
 ### [Topological Correctness Guarantees](1796-topological-correctness-guarantees.md)
-Topological Correctness Guarantees is a family of isosurface extraction methods designed to resolve the topological ambiguities inherent in standard Marching Cubes [774-marching-cubes.md]. Standard Marching Cubes uses linear interpolation, which can produce incorrect connectivity (e.g., disconnected components or holes) in ambiguous cases.
+Topological Correctness Guarantees is a family of isosurface extraction methods designed to resolve the topological ambiguities inherent in standard Marching Cubes [774-marching-cubes.md]. Standard Marching Cubes uses linear interpolation, which can produce incorrect connectivity (e.g., disconnected components or holes) in ambiguous cases. Choose it when strict topological validity is required for downstream simulation or analysis.
 
 ### [Node Mesh Generation](2112-node-mesh-generation.md)
-Node Mesh Generation constructs the explicit geometric vertex and face data for a voxel grid by defining the 8 corner vertices and 12 triangular faces of each voxel cube. It differs from topological extraction methods like Marching Cubes by generating a full mesh of the voxel structure.
+Node Mesh Generation constructs the explicit geometric vertex and face data for a voxel grid by defining the 8 corner vertices and 12 triangular faces of each voxel cube. It differs from topological extraction methods like Marching Cubes by generating a full mesh of the voxel structure. Choose it when a complete voxel representation is needed rather than a smooth isosurface.
 
 ### [Voxel Chunk Meshing with Culling](2523-voxel-chunk-meshing-with-culling.md)
-Voxel Chunk Meshing with Culling generates a static triangle mesh (typically quads) for a 3D voxel chunk by iterating through every voxel and emitting geometry only for faces adjacent to empty space or the chunk boundary. It solves the problem of excessive geometry and draw calls in large voxel worlds.
+Voxel Chunk Meshing with Culling generates a static triangle mesh (typically quads) for a 3D voxel chunk by iterating through every voxel and emitting geometry only for faces adjacent to empty space or the chunk boundary. It solves the problem of excessive geometry and draw calls in large voxel worlds. Choose it for real-time voxel engines where performance and draw call reduction are priorities.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, as each voxel is processed independently [S5][S13, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the input field and $O(T)$ for the output mesh, where $T$ is the number of triangles. The lookup tables are constant size (256 entries) [S84, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The algorithm is computationally intensive for large datasets due to the high triangle count (up to 5 triangles per voxel) [S1, unofficial]. Modern implementations often run on GPUs for parallel processing [S1, unofficial][S35].
-*   **Strengths:** Produces smooth, continuous surfaces; well-understood; efficient for uniform grids [S3, S22, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Suffers from topological ambiguities; generates high polygon counts; sensitive to noise in the scalar field; requires pre-computation of the mesh [S1, S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When a smooth, watertight mesh is required from a uniform grid scalar field, and the isovalue is fixed or changes infrequently [S3, S22, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When the data is non-uniform, when topological correctness is critical without post-processing, or when real-time updates with varying isovales are needed (consider direct volume rendering or GPU-based dynamic extraction) [S1, S3, unofficial].
